      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Current research indicates that the globalist system is increasingly shifting from a framework of mutual trade benefits to one that prioritizes corporate interests and market efficiency over national self-determination and social welfare. Data suggests that while trade agreements routinely strengthen economic stability in large economies, they systematically restrict smaller or developing nations' ability to enforce progressive environmental or labor standards, effectively creating a regulatory environment hostile to local priorities. While the system offers some transitional leverage for emerging economies, the net outcome at the current tipping point appears to be a structural erosion of democratic sovereignty in favor of entrenched corporate power. Ultimately, many analysts agree that the mechanism is not merely unbalanced but is explicitly designed to prioritize global market functionality, often at the direct expense of individual citizens' rights and national autonomy.\n\nI promise this will be short and accurate.\n*Strong pro-globalization* (But - manageable) **(3)**\n*Strong anti-globalization* (Anti-inefficiency) **(7)**\n\n**Summary:**\nThe evidence suggests the globalist model is fundamentally biased toward corporate interests and established powers, effectively functionally overriding national laws when those laws contradict trade agreements. While proponents argue this creates necessary market stability, recent outcomes show a clear trend where nations face severe penalties for prioritizing",
